  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nOverall little change seen in latest deterministic and ensemble \nguidance. Broad upper ridging over the southwest states keeps a \nmodest northwest flow pattern in place over the central Plains \nthrough the work week. The weak front that moved through overnight \nwas well south of the area at midday today with modest surface high \npressure over eastern Nebraska. Daytime temperatures and dewpoints \nappear to be warmer and drier, respectively, than NBM progs again \ntoday and have trended values in this manner over the next few \nperiods. Despite this, tonight brings another night of potential \nground fog development given light winds and clear skies as ground \nconditions remain damp. Have mentioned patchy fog formation for the \nbulk of the area with some support from guidance, especially the \nRAP. Any fog should dissipate by mid-morning Monday with southerly \nwinds returning and plenty of sun for another mild day and a warmer \nTuesday. \n\nAnother weak front looks to sink south into the area Wednesday with \nsome solutions producing light precipitation mainly just north of \nthe area Wednesday night into Thursday night as moisture profiles \ndeepen slightly. Local chances below 10 percent seem reasonable at \nthis time. The pattern changes somewhat late in the forecast with \nthe ridge push east and breaking down. Dry conditions still seem on \ntarget at this range with above normal temperatures.",
